WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.679
Alright, you've taken your first CCAF practice exam. Regardless of how you scored, listen to

2
00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:11.279
this. The fact that you're here reviewing means you're already doing this the right way. A lot of

3
00:00:11.279 --> 00:00:16.719
students take a practice exam, see a low score, and they panic. Some go back and re-watch the whole

4
00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:22.159
course from the beginning. Don't do that. Your time is far better spent in the practice and

5
00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:27.840
review cycle. That's where the real learning happens at this stage. And while you're processing

6
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.759
your exam results, take a minute to leave a Udemy review. I read and respond to every review,

7
00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:38.799
and it's a great way to tell me what I'm doing right or what I could improve on. In fact,

8
00:00:38.799 --> 00:00:44.240
if you have feedback for me that's more detailed, jump in there and send me a message because that

9
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.880
creates a dialogue between us so that I can find out exactly how to make the course better.

10
00:00:49.439 --> 00:00:53.840
This is a great place to do that because you've seen so much of the course at this point,

11
00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.400
and it helps me to improve as I move along. Here's my number one piece of advice. Lean into

12
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.559
the practice. You've spent serious time learning the CCAF material. Now commit the same energy to

13
00:01:06.559 --> 00:01:12.800
practicing it. Each practice exam should take you under two hours. They're built for focused sessions,

14
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.319
not marathons. Even if you scored well on the first one, don't skip the rest of the practice process.

15
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:24.239
Strong scores on an easier exam doesn't mean you're ready for the real thing. The repetition

16
00:01:24.239 --> 00:01:29.360
is what builds the architectural judgment you'll need when the real exam throws an unfamiliar

17
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.959
scenario at you. If you want more reps, I've built a companion course with six additional

18
00:01:34.959 --> 00:01:40.559
practice exams that mirror the same style and reasoning, and they advance in difficulty as you

19
00:01:40.559 --> 00:01:47.040
go. The link is in the bonus resources at the bottom of this course. Now let's talk about how

20
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.879
to review. This is where most of the learning happens. Start with every answer you got wrong.

21
00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:58.480
Read the explanation. If you don't understand why the correct answer is the better architecture,

22
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.440
go back to that lesson and review it. Don't just accept the answer. Make sure you understand the

23
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:09.199
reasoning. Next, examine every answer where you weren't completely confident. Even if you got it

24
00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:14.960
right, if you were guessing, review that question too. Lucky guesses don't help you on exam day.

25
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.320
For the questions you answered confidently and correctly, leave them alone for now. Your review

26
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.839
time is valuable. Spend it on the gaps. That focused review is what closes the gap between

27
00:02:25.839 --> 00:02:31.919
knowing the material and actually deploying it under exam pressure. Looking ahead, the second

28
00:02:31.919 --> 00:02:36.960
practice exam gets a little bit harder. That's intentional. You'll encounter new scenario types

29
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.559
and deeper architectural trade-offs that require more nuanced judgment. Your score should improve

30
00:02:42.559 --> 00:02:47.839
from the first exam, but don't expect a dramatic jump. What matters more than the score is the type

31
00:02:47.839 --> 00:02:52.960
of mistakes you're making. Are you making the same mistakes you made on the first exam? Go deeper on

32
00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.919
those topics before the next practice. Are you making new mistakes on new material? That's

33
00:02:57.919 --> 00:03:03.759
actually a good sign. You're growing past your original gaps and you're finding new ones. That's

34
00:03:03.759 --> 00:03:09.520
the practice process working exactly as it should. Take the second exam when you're ready and bring

35
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.960
the same focused review approach. I'll see you after that one.

